**Important** — Blob URL is revoked before the new tab can read it

**What:** `window.open(blobUrl, '_blank')` returns as soon as the tab is opened, but the new tab fetches the blob URL asynchronously after the current task yields. `URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl)` runs synchronously on the very next line, destroying the mapping before the popup has necessarily fetched it.

**Why it matters:** Intermittently produces a dead or blank tab when a user clicks Open. Firefox and Safari are the reliable failures; Chrome's grace period is an implementation detail rather than a contract, so it fails there too under load. The `handleDownload` path two functions up is fine — revoking right after `anchor.click()` is the established anchor-download pattern (`QueryRankingsExportButton.tsx:88-93`, `ExportPanel.tsx:74-80`) because the click consumes the URL synchronously. A navigation does not.

This is exactly the kind of thing only a runtime check catches, and the MR's test plan records that no runtime verification was performed.

**Recommendation:**
```ts
window.open(blobUrl, '_blank');
// The new tab fetches the blob asynchronously after this task yields —
// revoking now races it and can leave a blank tab.
setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl), 60_000);
```
(or revoke from the popup's `load` event if you open a `window` reference and can attach to it.)

Confidence: 75/100 | Found by: Codebase Reviewer (OmniForge)

**Minor** — New failure paths are invisible to the user

**What:** Both new failure paths report via `console.error` only — this one in `handleOpen`, and the `getAccessTokenSilently` catch in `RegeneratePdf.tsx`.

**Why it matters:** On a 401 or a token-refresh failure the user clicks Open and nothing visibly happens; the Regenerate modal renders an empty box. Since this MR is what introduces the possibility of these failures (the endpoints were previously open), the silent-failure path ships in the same change. Repo convention is sonner — `import { toast } from 'sonner'`, global `<Toaster />` already in `App.tsx` — and the neighbouring pattern is `toast.error(...)` on export failures (`QueryRankingsExportButton.tsx:96-99`).

**Recommendation:**
```ts
import { toast } from 'sonner';
// ...
} catch (error) {
    console.error('Error opening the file', error);
    toast.error('Could not open the report. Please try again.');
}
```

Confidence: 70/100 | Found by: Codebase Reviewer (OmniForge)

Fixed in commit 01a2fb44c: the revoke in `handleOpen` now defers 60s instead of running synchronously after `window.open`, so the new tab can no longer lose the race for the blob URL.

```ts
window.open(blobUrl, '_blank');
// The new tab fetches the blob asynchronously after this task yields —
// revoking now races it and can leave a blank tab.
setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl), 60_000);
```

`handleDownload`'s synchronous revoke is deliberately untouched — `anchor.click()` consumes the URL synchronously, so no race exists on that path. The deferred revoke matches the idiom already in `ImprovePanel.tsx:272`.

